Create one full-screen visual redesign mockup of the existing Urkaaaz match
HUD. The attached screenshot is the source of truth for the current layout,
content, and painted battlefield. Keep the layout; make the HUD feel more
modern, lighter, and more refined without losing the game's warm fantasy
identity.

This is a visual direction proposal only. Do not implement Android UI, create
production assets, or alter gameplay.

## Reference

Attach the current Urkaaaz match-screen screenshot supplied with this prompt.
Preserve its battlefield, biome, camera framing, fortresses, catapults, units,
terrain, and visible projectile/gameplay state. Use the screenshot as the
literal composition and content reference, not merely as inspiration.

## Output

- Create exactly one landscape mockup: `match_hud_redesign_v3_mockup.png`.
- Use a 1920 x 912 canvas, matching the reference's wide landscape format.
- Show the complete match screen at once, without a device frame, presentation
  board, extra views, or alternate states.
- Keep text, icons, values, bars, and controls visually separable from the
  battlefield. This is not a flattened production background or a reusable
  asset sheet.
- Present the mockup for review only. Do not integrate it into Android or
  replace any existing assets before approval.

## Preserve the layout and information

Do not move, resize, reorder, add, or remove the existing HUD groups and
controls. Preserve their current proportions and relationships:

- Top: BLUE fortress health at left; timer, wind, and one pause control in the
  center; RED fortress health at right.
- Above the bottom dock: one fixed, centered message strip.
- Bottom dock, left to right: Supply/units/orders, ammunition, spells/Mana.
- Keep Supply at the far left of its group and Mana at the far right of the
  spell group.
- Keep the current game content, labels, values, icons, health colors, and
  states shown in the screenshot. Do not invent game data or substitute
  placeholder controls.
- Keep the battlefield dominant and unobstructed. Do not modify the terrain,
  art, camera, units, fortresses, catapults, or projectile paths.

## Visual direction: modern Urkaaaz fantasy

The current HUD's layout is acceptable; its presentation feels too heavy.
Reduce the visual weight of the frames and repeated boxes rather than changing
the underlying composition.

- Keep the painted fantasy character of Urkaaaz: warm metal, restrained brass
  highlights, and warm ivory text. Do not turn the interface into generic
  mobile strategy UI.
- Use a consistent charcoal surface matching the existing Loadout footer:
  `#1A1A1A` at approximately 80% opacity (`#CC` alpha). Apply this same surface
  treatment to all six main HUD panels and the message strip. Do not make it
  opaque, pure black, differently tinted by section, or textured enough to
  compete with the battlefield.
- Replace the thick, bright, double gold bevels with a single fine bronze-gold
  edge and a restrained inner highlight. Keep corners softly rounded and the
  frame silhouette clean. No stacked borders, deep bevels, bulky ornaments,
  large rivets, spikes, or glowing outlines.
- Visually connect the three bottom groups as one lightweight dock. Use subtle
  separators and consistent alignment instead of three heavy, individually
  boxed panels. Keep the group order and boundaries immediately legible.
- Make the top health cards and center timer rail slimmer and calmer while
  preserving their alignment and relative sizes. The timer remains the focal
  point within the top HUD, not a large ornamental plaque.
- Reduce strong texture and contrast inside UI surfaces. Let the existing
  painterly battlefield provide the visual richness.
- Use clean, readable typography for labels and counts. Retain a restrained
  fantasy display character only where it helps the game's identity; avoid
  tiny serif labels and decorative lettering that is hard to read.
- Reserve brighter gold for selected or primary states. Keep the existing
  BLUE/RED health semantics and blue Mana identity; do not tint every panel by
  team or resource.
- Make buttons and slots feel lighter than the surrounding panels. Use a
  clear, restrained selected state; do not outline every inactive item with a
  bright gold frame.
- Maintain comfortable touch-target spacing and legible text at the reference
  scale.

## Avoid

- Do not change the accepted HUD layout or gameplay information.
- No thick double metallic frames, heavy black slabs, multiple nested
  outlines, ornate fantasy plaques, excessive texture, bright gold everywhere,
  neon, sci-fi glass, military styling, or strong glow.
- No invented labels, icons, values, controls, resources, objectives, or
  gameplay states.
- No redesign of the battlefield, art, camera, or existing game assets.
- No app/device frame, logo, watermark, contact sheet, sprite sheet, or
  standalone panel assets.

## Review checklist

- The whole mockup retains the screenshot's HUD layout and battlefield.
- The HUD looks visibly lighter and more contemporary, while still belonging
  to Urkaaaz's painted fantasy world.
- All six main panels and the message strip use the same semi-transparent
  charcoal surface; slot and button states remain visually distinct without
  making every control gold-framed.
- The bottom dock reads as one coherent system with three clear groups.
- Text, icons, values, and active states remain legible at the target scale.
- Nothing in the HUD obscures important battlefield action.
